<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP to the Firebase Emulator Suite, and to NOTHING else.

  **This file is in the `debug` source set on purpose and must never move to
  `main`.** Android has denied cleartext by default since API 28, which is why
  the first sign-in against the emulator failed with

      [firebase_auth/unknown] Cleartext HTTP traffic to 127.0.0.1 not permitted

  — measured on the POCO F3, 2026-08-21. The emulator suite speaks plain HTTP
  and cannot be persuaded otherwise, so the choice is between allowing cleartext
  somewhere or not developing against it at all.

  What makes that safe is the *scope*, twice over:

  1. **Only these two hosts.** A blanket `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"`
     would allow plaintext to anywhere, which on an app carrying data about
     vulnerable people is not a trade worth making for developer convenience.
     Everything not listed here keeps the platform default: TLS or nothing.
  2. **Only debug builds.** A release build never merges this file, so the
     shipped app cannot send cleartext to anything at all — including these two.
     `test/android_manifest_test.dart` asserts that separation, because it is
     the kind of thing a well-meaning "move it to main so profile builds work
     too" would quietly undo.

  The two hosts are the two ways a device reaches the suite, and they are not
  interchangeable:

      127.0.0.1   a physical handset, through `adb reverse` over USB
      10.0.2.2    the API 36 AVD, whose alias that is for the host loopback

  Both are loopback-scoped by construction: neither can address a machine
  anywhere else on the network.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
